ttychatter.mailbot 0.4.1
=========================

ttychatter.mailbot is a conservative IMAP/sendmail or IMAP/SMTP transport
adapter for ttychatter. It watches unread mail, accepts only ALLOWED_FROM
senders, passes ordinary chat messages to ttychatter.python, and replies by
email.

The mailbot is deliberately not an OpenRouter client. It delegates explicit
model-cache updates, formatted model-list commands, session context, memory,
favorites, attachments, and chat behavior to ttychatter.python. Read-only cache
retrieval and cache search are handled locally from MODEL_CACHE_FILE.

Main features
-------------

    - IMAP BODY.PEEK[] inspection, so dry-run and skipped messages do not become
      read merely because the bot inspected them.
    - ALLOWED_FROM sender gate with exact case-insensitive address matching.
    - Ordinary email-to-ttychatter chat sessions with attachment pass-through.
    - Uniform 0.4 mail syntax:

          tc:CONFIG
          tc:MODELS
          tc:CMD
          tc:command args
          tc:{session-name}
          tc:{new}

    - Config export/update by email through tc:CONFIG.
    - Read-only model-cache retrieval through tc:MODELS, with the existing
      models-cache.json returned by email when the cache can be attached.
    - Explicit model-cache refresh through tc:update-models.
    - Cached model-list search through tc:model-search TEXT.
    - Mailbot control commands through bare tc:command subjects or through
      tc:CMD with the command on the first nonempty body line.
    - Session listing with head/tail previews from each .log file.
    - Session search, rename, and delete commands.
    - Remote retrieval of the mailbot manpage.
    - Safe allowlisted ttychatter utility commands over email.

Files
-----

Install or ship these files together:

    ttychatter.mailbot
    ttychatter.mailbot.1
    README
    MAILBOT-WALKTHROUGH
    CHANGELOG

Typical local commands
----------------------

    ttychatter.mailbot --doctor
    ttychatter.mailbot --list-candidates
    ttychatter.mailbot --sessions
    ttychatter.mailbot --manpage
    ttychatter.mailbot --model-cache >/tmp/models-cache.json
    ttychatter.mailbot --model-search claude
    ttychatter.mailbot --update-models --dry-run
    ttychatter.mailbot --config
    ttychatter.mailbot --raw-config
    ttychatter.mailbot --once --dry-run
    ttychatter.mailbot --once

Mail subject grammar
--------------------

Control subjects are bare. Chat-session subjects are braced.

    tc:CONFIG                    Export or apply mailbot config
    tc:MODELS                    Attach existing cached models-cache.json; no refresh
    tc:MODELS TEXT               Search existing cached model list; no refresh
    tc:CMD                       Run first nonempty body line as a command
    tc:command args              Run a mailbot command directly
    tc:{session-name}            Send body to a ttychatter session
    tc:{new}                     Start a new chat using ttychatter's own default
                                  session-YYYY-MM-DD-HH-MM-SS name

Examples:

    Subject: tc:CONFIG
    Subject: tc:MODELS
    Subject: tc:MODELS claude
    Subject: tc:sessions
    Subject: tc:manpage
    Subject: tc:search gpg-agent
    Subject: tc:delete{old-session}
    Subject: tc:model-search claude
    Subject: tc:model-list --all --sort context
    Subject: tc:routers --model-type chat --require-tokens
    Subject: tc:{mailbot-test}
    Subject: tc:{new}

The tc:CMD form is useful when the command or its arguments are easier to put
in the body:

    Subject: tc:CMD

    search gpg-agent

Legacy compatibility
--------------------

These older subjects are still accepted for compatibility:

    tc:{CONFIG}
    tc:{MAILBOT}
    tc:{MAILBOT:command args}

New mail should use the 0.4 bare control form. Braces are now reserved for chat
sessions, except for those compatibility aliases.

Email control commands
----------------------

All commands require an ALLOWED_FROM sender. Commands are allowlisted and are
not shell-expanded.

    help
        Return the command summary.

    manpage | manual | get-manpage | fetch-manpage
        Attach ttychatter.mailbot.1 to the reply. The bot uses
        MAILBOT_MANPAGE_FILE or another installed external manpage when present,
        and falls back to the embedded manpage otherwise.

    config
        Attach the public/redacted mailbot config. Equivalent to tc:CONFIG
        without a config attachment.

    sessions | list | list-sessions | session-list
        List session logs newest first, with modified time, size, line count,
        and short previews from the head and tail of each log.

    search TEXT
        Search all session logs.

    search-session SESSION TEXT
        Search one session log.

    rename-session OLD NEW
        Rename a session log after sanitizing names. Dry-run does not rename.

    delete-session SESSION
    delete{SESSION}
    rm{SESSION}
        Delete one session log from SESSION_DIR after resolving it as a
        sanitized .log name. Dry-run does not delete.

    model-cache | cache-models | cached-models | models-cache | model-json
        Attach the existing MODEL_CACHE_FILE as models-cache.json. This command
        is read-only and never contacts OpenRouter. tc:MODELS is the preferred
        subject shorthand.

    tc:MODELS TEXT
    model-search TEXT | search-models TEXT | models-search TEXT | model-list-search TEXT | find-model TEXT | find-models TEXT | grep-models TEXT
        Search the existing cached model list for TEXT. This command is
        read-only and never contacts OpenRouter.

    update-models | update-model-list | update-model-cache | refresh-models
        Run ttychatter.python --update-models and attach models-cache.json when
        the resulting cache is present and small enough to send. This is the
        explicit network refresh command.

    model-list [FLAGS]
    list-models [FLAGS]
    models-list [FLAGS]
    show-models [FLAGS]
    models [FLAGS]
        Run ttychatter.python --models with only safe model-list flags. The
        subject tc:MODELS with no flags is reserved for read-only cache
        retrieval; use tc:model-list for a formatted model-list command.

    routers [FLAGS]
        Run ttychatter.python --routers with the same safe model-list flags.

Safe model-list flags are:

    --all
    --sort VALUE
    --modellist-sort-tokens
    --min-input-tokens N
    --min-output-tokens N
    --hide-preview
    --show-preview
    --require-tokens
    --allow-missing-tokens
    --model-type VALUE

Additional ttychatter utility commands:

    favorites | faves
    favorite-model MODEL
    unfavorite-model MODEL | unbookmark-model MODEL
    test-model MODEL
    ttyconfig | ttyconfig-summary
    ttydoctor | doctor
    credits
    show-memory SESSION
    clear-memory SESSION
    tty-set KEY VALUE
    tty-unset KEY

tty-set refuses OPENROUTER_API_KEY and API_KEY by email; set secrets locally.

Config-over-email
-----------------

Send an allowed message with this subject:

    tc:CONFIG

No config attachment means: send me the current public config as an attachment
named ttychatter-mailbot-config.txt. Plaintext password values are redacted.

An edited text config attachment means: replace the current config after making
a timestamped backup. Dry-run never writes config.

The legacy subject tc:{CONFIG} still works, but the canonical subject is now
tc:CONFIG.

Chat sessions
-------------

Use braces for ordinary ttychatter chat sessions:

    tc:{project-name}
    tc:{mailbot-test}

The message body becomes the ttychatter prompt. Attachments are saved into the
mailbot work directory and passed to ttychatter.python with --attach when
PROCESS_ATTACHMENTS=1.

The special subject:

    tc:{new}

passes no session argument to ttychatter.python. That lets ttychatter.python use
its regular default new-session behavior and create a session name like:

    session-2026-05-30-01-02-03

Important config keys
---------------------

    IMAP_HOST=localhost
    IMAP_PORT=993
    IMAP_SSL=1
    IMAP_USER=user@example.net
    IMAP_PASSWORD_GPG_FILE=~/.config/ttychatter/mailbot/imap-password.gpg
    IMAP_MAILBOX=INBOX

    MAIL_FROM=ttychatter@example.net
    ALLOWED_FROM=you@example.net

    SEND_METHOD=sendmail
    SENDMAIL=/usr/sbin/sendmail

    TTYCHATTER_COMMAND=/home/user/bin/ttychatter.python
    TTYCHATTER_EXTRA_ARGS=
    SESSION_DIR=~/.local/share/ttychatter/openrouter/sessions
    ATTACHMENT_DIR=~/.local/share/ttychatter/openrouter/attachments
    MODEL_CACHE_FILE=~/.config/ttychatter/openrouter/models-cache.json

    SUBJECT_PREFIX=tc
    CONFIG_SUBJECT_TOKEN=CONFIG
    MODELS_SUBJECT_TOKEN=MODELS
    COMMAND_SUBJECT_TOKEN=CMD
    MAILBOT_SUBJECT_TOKEN=MAILBOT
    MAILBOT_MANPAGE_FILE=

    TTYCHATTER_CONTROL_TIMEOUT=180
    SESSION_LIST_HEAD_LINES=3
    SESSION_LIST_TAIL_LINES=3
    SESSION_LIST_MAX_SESSIONS=50
    SESSION_PREVIEW_LINE_CHARS=220
    SESSION_SEARCH_MAX_MATCHES=100
    MODEL_SEARCH_MAX_MATCHES=100
    MAX_COMMAND_OUTPUT_BYTES=200000
    MAX_OUTGOING_ATTACHMENT_BYTES=8388608

MAILBOT_SUBJECT_TOKEN remains only for legacy tc:{MAILBOT:...} subjects.

Config path diagnostics
-----------------------

    ttychatter.mailbot --config

prints the parsed/effective config, not a byte-for-byte copy of the file. It
omits comments and unknown keys and reports password values only as set or
unset. It also prints PROCESS_USER, HOME, TTYCHATTER_MAILBOT_CONFIG,
XDG_CONFIG_HOME, CONFIG_FILE, CONFIG_EXISTS, CONFIG_READABLE, and CONFIG_MODE.

To see the actual loaded file with plaintext password lines redacted:

    ttychatter.mailbot --raw-config

If --config looks incomplete while the file you edited looks complete, compare
CONFIG_FILE against the path you edited. Also compare:

    command -v ttychatter.mailbot
    ttychatter.mailbot --version
    ./ttychatter.mailbot --version

TTYCHATTER_MAILBOT_CONFIG may set the default config path for rc scripts or
cron jobs. --config-file PATH overrides both the default and that environment
variable. If TTYCHATTER_MAILBOT_CONFIG is unset, XDG_CONFIG_HOME is honored
before ~/.config.

Cron notes
----------

Cron usually has a smaller PATH than an interactive shell. If a log shows:

    env: python3: No such file or directory

invoke the mailbot through Python explicitly and set TTYCHATTER_COMMAND to an
absolute Python invocation:

    /usr/local/bin/python3 /home/r94/bin/ttychatter.mailbot --config-file /home/r94/.config/ttychatter/mailbot/config --once

    TTYCHATTER_COMMAND=/usr/local/bin/python3 /home/r94/bin/ttychatter.python

Run the cron job as the mailbot user, not as root, unless the mailbot is really
configured to use root's HOME, config, GPG keyring, and state directories.

GPG password troubleshooting
----------------------------

When IMAP_PASSWORD is unset and IMAP_PASSWORD_GPG_FILE exists, --once must
decrypt that file before it can connect to IMAP. A failure such as:

    gpg: problem with the agent: Permission denied
    gpg: decryption failed: Bad session key

means gpg-agent or pinentry could not unlock the configured password file, or
the wrong passphrase is cached. Test the same decrypt as the mailbot user:

    export GPG_TTY=$(tty)
    gpgconf --launch gpg-agent
    gpg-connect-agent updatestartuptty /bye
    gpg --decrypt ~/.config/ttychatter/mailbot/imap-password.gpg >/dev/null

If a bad passphrase is cached, run:

    gpgconf --kill gpg-agent

Also check ownership and permissions for ~/.gnupg and the configured .gpg
password file. Do not diagnose this by switching to root unless the service is
actually configured to run as root; root changes HOME, GNUPGHOME, and the
gpg-agent socket. For unattended daemon runs, gpg-agent must already have the
passphrase cached; otherwise use a protected plaintext IMAP_PASSWORD line, a
TTYCHATTER_IMAP_PASSWORD environment override, or another local secret manager.

If root or another user changes the error to:

    IMAP error: LOGIN command error: BAD

first confirm that the intended config is being read:

    ttychatter.mailbot --config
    ttychatter.mailbot --doctor

A BAD login command usually means an empty IMAP_USER, a password containing a
carriage return or newline, or a different user/HOME than the configured
mailbot account. The mailbot rejects those local login-value problems before
sending LOGIN.

Security
--------

Set ALLOWED_FROM and keep it narrow. The wildcard * exists for temporary local
testing only. Do not run a production mailbot that accepts arbitrary senders.

Email is not a secret channel. Do not use tty-set for API keys or passwords.
Use ttychatter.python --set-api-key locally, and use ttychatter.mailbot
--set-imap-password --gpg or --set-smtp-password --gpg for mail passwords.

The mailbot does not shell-expand email commands. Control commands are
allowlisted. TTYCHATTER_EXTRA_ARGS is appended only to ordinary loopback chat
calls, not to utility/control calls.

Manpage lookup
--------------

The manpage command and --manpage use an external file first and an embedded
fallback second. The lookup order is:

    TTYCHATTER_MAILBOT_MANPAGE_FILE from environment, when set
    MAILBOT_MANPAGE_FILE from config
    ~/.config/ttychatter/mailbot/ttychatter.mailbot.1
    ~/.config/ttychatter/mailbot/ttychatter.mailbot.1.gz
    directory beside the script/ttychatter.mailbot.1
    directory beside the script/ttychatter.mailbot.1.gz
    directory beside argv[0]/ttychatter.mailbot.1
    directory beside argv[0]/ttychatter.mailbot.1.gz
    $XDG_DATA_HOME/man/man1/ttychatter.mailbot.1
    $XDG_DATA_HOME/man/man1/ttychatter.mailbot.1.gz
    ~/.local/share/man/man1/ttychatter.mailbot.1
    ~/.local/share/man/man1/ttychatter.mailbot.1.gz
    /usr/local/share/man/man1/ttychatter.mailbot.1
    /usr/local/share/man/man1/ttychatter.mailbot.1.gz
    /usr/local/man/man1/ttychatter.mailbot.1
    /usr/local/man/man1/ttychatter.mailbot.1.gz
    /usr/share/man/man1/ttychatter.mailbot.1
    /usr/share/man/man1/ttychatter.mailbot.1.gz
    current working directory/ttychatter.mailbot.1
    current working directory/ttychatter.mailbot.1.gz

For a normal install, name the file ttychatter.mailbot.1. For an arbitrary
location or filename, set:

    MAILBOT_MANPAGE_FILE=/path/to/ttychatter.mailbot.1

or set the environment override:

    TTYCHATTER_MAILBOT_MANPAGE_FILE=/path/to/ttychatter.mailbot.1

Version notes
-------------

0.4.1
    Corrects tc:MODELS and model-cache semantics. tc:MODELS/model-cache now
    attach the already-downloaded MODEL_CACHE_FILE without refreshing it or
    contacting OpenRouter. The explicit refresh command remains update-models.
    Adds cached model-list search through model-search/search-models. Adds
    delete-session plus compact tc:delete{SESSION} and tc:rm{SESSION}.

0.4.0
    Replaces the mixed tc:{CONFIG} and tc:{MAILBOT:...} public syntax with bare
    control subjects: tc:CONFIG, tc:MODELS, tc:CMD, and tc:command args. Braces
    are reserved for chat sessions: tc:{session-name} and tc:{new}. The legacy
    braced control subjects remain accepted for compatibility.

0.3.4
    Flattened the preferred command syntax toward tc:CONFIG, tc:MODELS, and
    tc:command forms. 0.4.0 keeps that direction, adds tc:CMD, adds configurable
    MODELS_SUBJECT_TOKEN and COMMAND_SUBJECT_TOKEN, and changes tc:{new} to let
    ttychatter.python create the session name itself.

0.3.3
    Makes config-path mistakes visible with --config and --raw-config,
    supports TTYCHATTER_MAILBOT_CONFIG and XDG_CONFIG_HOME in config path
    resolution, and accepts shell-style export KEY=VALUE config lines.

0.3.2
    Adds --raw-config, prints process user/HOME/config-file status in --config,
    and accepts TTYCHATTER_MAILBOT_CONFIG as a default config-path override.

0.3.1
    Improves GPG password-file diagnostics, sets GPG_TTY automatically when a
    controlling terminal is available, documents gpg-agent/pinentry fixes, and
    adds local IMAP login-value validation for empty user/password and CR/LF
    syntax traps.

0.3.0
    Adds remote model-cache refresh with cache attachment, session listings with
    head/tail previews, session search/rename, mailbot manpage retrieval by
    email with external manpage lookup, local --sessions, local --manpage, and
    local --update-models.

0.2.1
    ALLOWED_FROM matching is exact, case-insensitive, and may contain comma,
    semicolon, or whitespace separated addresses. A configured sender is no
    longer rejected merely because it is also MAIL_FROM.

0.2.0
    Uses IMAP BODY.PEEK[] for inspection so dry-run and skipped messages do not
    become read merely because the bot looked at them.
